9	## Anatomy of a Future
10	
11	Everything in async Rust ultimately implements this trait:
12	
13	```rust
14	pub trait Future {
15	    type Output;
16	
17	    fn poll(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output>;
18	}
19	
20	pub enum Poll<T> {
21	    Ready(T),   // The future has completed with value T
22	    Pending,    // The future is not ready yet — call me back later
23	}
24	```
25	
26	That's it. A `Future` is anything that can be *polled* — asked "are you done yet?" — and responds with either "yes, here's the result" or "not yet, I'll wake you up when I'm ready."
27	
28	### Output, poll(), Context, Waker
29	
30	```mermaid
31	sequenceDiagram
32	    participant E as Executor
33	    participant F as Future (Task)
34	    participant OS as Operating System<br/>(e.g., epoll/kqueue)
35	    participant R as Reactor (Runtime)
36	
37	    E->>F: Calls poll(cx)
38	    Note right of F: Future attempts operation
39	    F->>OS: Syscall (e.g., read TCP socket)
40	    OS-->>F: Returns Error: Not Ready
41	    
42	    F->>R: Registers: (Waker)
43	    F-->>E: Returns Poll::Pending
44	    Note left of E: Task is moved out<br/>of run queue
45	
46	    E->>E: (Executor runs other tasks OR sleeps)
47	    R->>OS: epoll_wait() / Polls OS for events
48	
49	    Note right of OS: (Sometime Later) New data arrives
50	    OS-->>R: Wakes Reactor: data is NOW READY
51	    
52	    R->>R: Reactor finds Waker
53	    R->>E: Calls Waker::wake()
54	    Note right of E: Task is pushed back<br/>to Executor's run queue
55	
56	    E->>F: Calls poll(cx) again
57	    Note right of F: Future attempts operation again
58	    F->>OS: Syscall (e.g., read TCP socket)
59	    OS-->>F: Success: Returns Data Buffer
60	    F-->>E: Returns Poll::Ready(Data)
61	```
62	
63	Let's break down each piece:
64	
65	```rust
66	use std::future::Future;
67	use std::pin::Pin;
68	use std::task::{Context, Poll};
69	
70	// A future that returns 42 immediately
71	struct Ready42;
72	
73	impl Future for Ready42 {
74	    type Output = i32; // What the future eventually produces
75	
76	    fn poll(self: Pin<&mut Self>, _cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<i32> {
77	        Poll::Ready(42) // Always ready — no waiting
78	    }
79	}
80	```
81	
82	**The components**:
83	- **`Output`** — the type of value produced when the future completes
84	- **`poll()`** — called by the executor to check progress; returns `Ready(value)` or `Pending`
85	- **`Pin<&mut Self>`** — ensures the future won't be moved in memory (we'll cover why in Ch. 4)
86	- **`Context`** — carries the `Waker` so the future can signal the executor when it's ready to make progress
87	
88	### The Waker Contract
89	
90	The `Waker` is the callback mechanism. When a future returns `Pending`, it *must* arrange for `waker.wake()` to be called later — otherwise the executor will never poll it again and the program hangs.
91	
92	```rust
93	use std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};
94	use std::pin::Pin;
95	use std::future::Future;
96	use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
97	use std::thread;
98	use std::time::Duration;
99	
100	/// A future that completes after a delay (toy implementation)
101	struct Delay {
102	    completed: Arc<Mutex<bool>>,
103	    waker_stored: Arc<Mutex<Option<Waker>>>,
104	    duration: Duration,
105	    started: bool,
106	}
107	
108	impl Delay {
109	    fn new(duration: Duration) -> Self {
110	        Delay {
111	            completed: Arc::new(Mutex::new(false)),
112	            waker_stored: Arc::new(Mutex::new(None)),
113	            duration,
114	            started: false,
115	        }
116	    }
117	}
118	
119	impl Future for Delay {
120	    type Output = ();
121	
122	    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {
123	        // Check if already completed before storing waker
124	        if *self.completed.lock().unwrap() {
125	            return Poll::Ready(());
126	        }
127	
128	        // Store the waker - executor may pass a new one on each poll
129	        *self.waker_stored.lock().unwrap() = Some(cx.waker().clone());
130	
131	        // Start the background timer on first poll
132	        if !self.started {
133	            self.started = true;
134	            let completed = Arc::clone(&self.completed);
135	            let waker = Arc::clone(&self.waker_stored);
136	            let duration = self.duration;
137	
138	            thread::spawn(move || {
139	                thread::sleep(duration);
140	                *completed.lock().unwrap() = true;
141	
142	                // CRITICAL: wake the executor so it polls us again
143	                if let Some(w) = waker.lock().unwrap().take() {
144	                    w.wake(); // "Hey executor, I'm ready — poll me again!"
145	                }
146	            });
147	        }
148	
149	        // Double-check completion after storing waker (handles race condition)
150	        if *self.completed.lock().unwrap() {
151	            return Poll::Ready(());
152	        }
153	
154	        Poll::Pending // Not done yet
155	    }
156	}
157	```
158	
159	> **Key insight**: In C#, the TaskScheduler handles waking automatically.
160	> In Rust, **you** (or the I/O library you use) are responsible for calling
161	> `waker.wake()`. Forget it, and your program silently hangs.
162	
163	### Exercise: Implement a CountdownFuture
164	
165	<details>
166	<summary>🏋️ Exercise (click to expand)</summary>
167	
168	**Challenge**: Implement a `CountdownFuture` that counts down from N to 0, printing the current count each time it's polled. When it reaches 0, it completes with `Ready("Liftoff!")`.
169	
170	*Hint*: The future needs to store the current count and decrement it on each poll. Remember to always re-register the waker!
171	
172	<details>
173	<summary>🔑 Solution</summary>
174	
175	```rust
176	use std::future::Future;
177	use std::pin::Pin;
178	use std::task::{Context, Poll};
179	
180	struct CountdownFuture {
181	    count: u32,
182	}
183	
184	impl CountdownFuture {
185	    fn new(start: u32) -> Self {
186	        CountdownFuture { count: start }
187	    }
188	}
189	
190	impl Future for CountdownFuture {
191	    type Output = &'static str;
192	
193	    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {
194	        if self.count == 0 {
195	            println!("Liftoff!");
196	            Poll::Ready("Liftoff!")
197	        } else {
198	            println!("{}...", self.count);
199	            self.count -= 1;
200	            cx.waker().wake_by_ref(); // Schedule re-poll immediately
201	            Poll::Pending
202	        }
203	    }
204	}
205	```
206	
207	**Key takeaway**: This future is polled once per count. Each time it returns `Pending`, it immediately wakes itself to be polled again. In production, you'd use a timer instead of busy-polling.
208	
209	</details>
210	</details>
211	
212	> **Key Takeaways — The Future Trait**
213	> - `Future::poll()` returns `Poll::Ready(value)` or `Poll::Pending`
214	> - A future must register a `Waker` before returning `Pending` — the executor uses it to know when to re-poll
215	> - `Pin<&mut Self>` guarantees the future won't be moved in memory (needed for self-referential state machines — see Ch 4)
