55	### std::thread::sleep vs tokio::time::sleep
56	
57	```rust
58	// ❌ WRONG: Blocks the executor thread for 5 seconds
59	async fn bad_delay() {
60	    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(5)); // Thread can't poll anything else!
61	}
62	
63	// ✅ CORRECT: Yields to the executor, other tasks can run
64	async fn good_delay() {
65	    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(5)).await; // Non-blocking!
66	}
67	```
68	
69	### Holding MutexGuard Across .await
70	
71	```rust
72	use std::sync::Mutex; // std Mutex — NOT async-aware
73	
74	// ⚠️ RISKY: MutexGuard held across .await
75	async fn bad_mutex(data: &Mutex<Vec<String>>) {
76	    let mut guard = data.lock().unwrap();
77	    guard.push("item".into());
78	    some_io().await; // Guard is held here — blocks other threads from locking!
79	    guard.push("another".into());
80	}
81	// NOTE: This compiles! std::sync::MutexGuard is !Send, but the compiler only
82	// enforces Send on the Future when you pass it to something that requires it
83	// (e.g., tokio::spawn). Calling bad_mutex(...).await directly compiles fine.
84	// However, tokio::spawn(bad_mutex(data)) will fail with a Send bound error.
85	```
86	
87	**Why this is usually a problem** — but not always:
88	
89	Holding a `std::sync::Mutex` across `.await` blocks the **OS thread** for the
90	duration of the I/O, preventing the executor from polling other tasks on that
91	thread. For short critical sections this is wasteful; for long I/O it's a
92	performance trap.
93	
94	**However**, there are legitimate cases where you *must* hold a lock across an
95	`.await` — the same way a database transaction holds a lock between read and
96	commit. Dropping and re-acquiring the lock introduces a **TOCTOU (time-of-check
97	to time-of-use) race**: another task can modify the data between your two
98	critical sections. The right fix depends on the use case:
99	
100	```rust
101	// OPTION 1: Scope the guard — works when operations are independent
102	async fn scoped_mutex(data: &Mutex<Vec<String>>) {
103	    {
104	        let mut guard = data.lock().unwrap();
105	        guard.push("item".into());
106	    } // Guard dropped here
107	    some_io().await; // Lock is released — other tasks can proceed
108	    {
109	        let mut guard = data.lock().unwrap();
110	        guard.push("another".into());
111	    }
112	}
113	// ⚠️ Careful: another task can lock + modify the Vec between the two sections.
114	//    This is fine if the two pushes are independent, but wrong if "another"
115	//    depends on state set by "item".
116	
117	// OPTION 2: Use tokio::sync::Mutex — holds lock across .await without
118	//           blocking the OS thread. Best when you need transactional
119	//           read-modify-write across an await point.
120	use tokio::sync::Mutex as AsyncMutex;
121	
122	async fn async_mutex(data: &AsyncMutex<Vec<String>>) {
123	    let mut guard = data.lock().await; // Async lock — doesn't block the thread
124	    guard.push("item".into());
125	    some_io().await; // OK — tokio Mutex guard is Send
126	    guard.push("another".into());
127	    // Guard held the whole time — no TOCTOU race, no thread blocked.
128	}
129	```
130	
131	> **When to use which Mutex**:
132	> - `std::sync::Mutex`: Short critical sections with no `.await` inside
133	> - `tokio::sync::Mutex`: When you need to hold the lock across `.await` points
134	>   (transactional semantics, TOCTOU avoidance)
135	> - `parking_lot::Mutex`: Drop-in `std` replacement, faster, smaller, still no `.await`
136	>
137	> **Rule of thumb**: Don't blindly split a critical section around an `.await`.
138	> Ask whether the two halves are truly independent. If they aren't — if the
139	> second half depends on state from the first — use `tokio::sync::Mutex` or
140	> redesign the data flow.
141	
142	### Cancellation Hazards
143	
144	Dropping a future cancels it — but this can leave things in an inconsistent state:
145	
146	```rust
147	// ❌ DANGEROUS: Resource leak on cancellation
148	async fn transfer(from: &Account, to: &Account, amount: u64) {
149	    from.debit(amount).await;  // If cancelled HERE...
150	    to.credit(amount).await;   // ...money vanishes!
151	}
152	
153	// ✅ SAFE: Make operations atomic or use compensation
154	async fn safe_transfer(from: &Account, to: &Account, amount: u64) -> Result<(), Error> {
155	    // Use a database transaction (all-or-nothing)
156	    let tx = db.begin_transaction().await?;
157	    tx.debit(from, amount).await?;
158	    tx.credit(to, amount).await?;
159	    tx.commit().await?; // Only commits if everything succeeded
160	    Ok(())
161	}
162	
163	// ✅ ALSO SAFE: Use tokio::select! with cancellation awareness
164	tokio::select! {
165	    result = transfer(from, to, amount) => {
166	        // Transfer completed
167	    }
168	    _ = shutdown_signal() => {
169	        // Don't cancel mid-transfer — let it finish
170	        // Or: roll back explicitly
171	    }
172	}
173	```
174	
175	### No Async Drop
176	
177	Rust's `Drop` trait is synchronous — you **cannot** `.await` inside `drop()`. This is a frequent source of confusion:
178	
179	```rust
180	struct DbConnection { /* ... */ }
181	
182	impl Drop for DbConnection {
183	    fn drop(&mut self) {
184	        // ❌ Can't do this — drop() is sync!
185	        // self.connection.shutdown().await;
186	
187	        // ✅ Workaround 1: Spawn a cleanup task (fire-and-forget)
188	        let conn = self.connection.take();
189	        tokio::spawn(async move {
190	            let _ = conn.shutdown().await;
191	        });
192	
193	        // ✅ Workaround 2: Use a synchronous close
194	        // self.connection.blocking_close();
195	    }
196	}
197	```
198	
199	**Best practice**: Provide an explicit `async fn close(self)` method and document that callers should use it. Rely on `Drop` only as a safety net, not the primary cleanup path.
200	
201	### select! Fairness and Starvation
202	
203	```rust
204	use tokio::sync::mpsc;
205	
206	// ❌ UNFAIR: busy_stream always wins, slow_stream starves
207	async fn unfair(mut fast: mpsc::Receiver<i32>, mut slow: mpsc::Receiver<i32>) {
208	    loop {
209	        tokio::select! {
210	            Some(v) = fast.recv() => println!("fast: {v}"),
211	            Some(v) = slow.recv() => println!("slow: {v}"),
212	            // If both are ready, tokio randomly picks one.
213	            // But if `fast` is ALWAYS ready, `slow` rarely gets polled.
214	        }
215	    }
216	}
217	
218	// ✅ FAIR: Use biased select or drain in batches
219	async fn fair(mut fast: mpsc::Receiver<i32>, mut slow: mpsc::Receiver<i32>) {
220	    loop {
221	        tokio::select! {
222	            biased; // Always check in order — explicit priority
223	
224	            Some(v) = slow.recv() => println!("slow: {v}"),  // Priority!
225	            Some(v) = fast.recv() => println!("fast: {v}"),
226	        }
227	    }
228	}
229	```
230	
231	### Accidental Sequential Execution
232	
233	```rust
234	// ❌ SEQUENTIAL: Takes 2 seconds total
235	async fn slow() {
236	    let a = fetch("url_a").await; // 1 second
237	    let b = fetch("url_b").await; // 1 second (waits for a to finish first!)
238	}
239	
240	// ✅ CONCURRENT: Takes 1 second total
241	async fn fast() {
242	    let (a, b) = tokio::join!(
243	        fetch("url_a"), // Both start immediately
244	        fetch("url_b"),
245	    );
246	}
247	
248	// ✅ ALSO CONCURRENT: Using let + join
249	async fn also_fast() {
250	    let fut_a = fetch("url_a"); // Create future (lazy — not started yet)
251	    let fut_b = fetch("url_b"); // Create future
252	    let (a, b) = tokio::join!(fut_a, fut_b); // NOW both run concurrently
253	}
254	```
255	
256	> **Trap**: `let a = fetch(url).await; let b = fetch(url).await;` is sequential!
257	> The second `.await` doesn't start until the first finishes. Use `join!` or
258	> `spawn` for concurrency.
259	
260	## Case Study: Debugging a Hung Production Service
261	
262	A real-world scenario: a service handles requests fine for 10 minutes, then stops responding. No errors in logs. CPU at 0%.
263	
264	**Diagnosis steps:**
265	
266	1. **Attach `tokio-console`** — reveals 200+ tasks stuck in `Pending` state
267	2. **Check task details** — all waiting on the same `Mutex::lock().await`
268	3. **Root cause** — one task held a `std::sync::MutexGuard` across an `.await` and panicked, poisoning the mutex. All other tasks now fail on `lock().unwrap()`
269	
270	**The fix:**
271	
272	| Before (broken) | After (fixed) |
273	|-----------------|---------------|
274	| `std::sync::Mutex` | `tokio::sync::Mutex` |
275	| `.lock().unwrap()` across `.await` | Scope lock before `.await` |
276	| No timeout on lock acquisition | `tokio::time::timeout(dur, mutex.lock())` |
277	| No recovery on poisoned mutex | `tokio::sync::Mutex` doesn't poison |
278	
279	**Prevention checklist:**
280	- [ ] Use `tokio::sync::Mutex` if the guard crosses any `.await`
281	- [ ] Add `#[tracing::instrument]` to async functions for span tracking
282	- [ ] Run `tokio-console` in staging to catch hung tasks early
283	- [ ] Add health check endpoints that verify task responsiveness
284	
285	<details>
286	<summary><strong>🏋️ Exercise: Spot the Bugs</strong> (click to expand)</summary>
287	
288	**Challenge**: Find all the async pitfalls in this code and fix them.
289	
290	```rust
291	use std::sync::Mutex;
292	
293	async fn process_requests(urls: Vec<String>) -> Vec<String> {
294	    let results = Mutex::new(Vec::new());
295	    
296	    for url in &urls {
297	        let response = reqwest::get(url).await.unwrap().text().await.unwrap();
298	        std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100)); // Rate limit
299	        let mut guard = results.lock().unwrap();
300	        guard.push(response);
301	        expensive_parse(&guard).await; // Parse all results so far
302	    }
303	    
304	    results.into_inner().unwrap()
305	}
306	```
307	
308	<details>
309	<summary>🔑 Solution</summary>
310	
311	**Bugs found:**
312	
313	1. **Sequential fetches** — URLs are fetched one at a time instead of concurrently
314	2. **`std::thread::sleep`** — Blocks the executor thread
315	3. **MutexGuard held across `.await`** — `guard` is alive when `expensive_parse` is awaited
316	4. **No concurrency** — Should use `join!` or `FuturesUnordered`
317	
318	```rust
319	use tokio::sync::Mutex;
320	use std::sync::Arc;
321	use futures::stream::{self, StreamExt};
322	
323	async fn process_requests(urls: Vec<String>) -> Vec<String> {
324	    // Fix 4: Process URLs concurrently with buffer_unordered
325	    let results: Vec<String> = stream::iter(urls)
326	        .map(|url| async move {
327	            let response = reqwest::get(&url).await.unwrap().text().await.unwrap();
328	            // Fix 2: Use tokio::time::sleep instead of std::thread::sleep
329	            tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100)).await;
330	            response
331	        })
332	        .buffer_unordered(10) // Up to 10 concurrent requests
333	        .collect()
334	        .await;
335	
336	    // Fix 3: Parse after collecting — no mutex needed at all!
337	    for result in &results {
338	        expensive_parse(result).await;
339	    }
340	
341	    results
342	}
343	```
344	
345	**Key takeaway**: Often you can restructure async code to eliminate mutexes entirely. Collect results with streams/join, then process. Simpler, faster, no deadlock risk.
346	
347	</details>
348	</details>
349	
350	---
351	
352	### Debugging Async Code
353	
354	Async stack traces are notoriously cryptic — they show the executor's poll loop rather than your logical call chain. Here are the essential debugging tools.
355	
356	#### tokio-console: Real-Time Task Inspector
357	
358	[tokio-console](https://github.com/tokio-rs/console) gives you an `htop`-like view of every spawned task: its state, poll duration, waker activity, and resource usage.
359	
360	```toml
361	# Cargo.toml
362	[dependencies]
363	console-subscriber = "0.4"
364	tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full", "tracing"] }
365	```
366	
367	```rust
368	#[tokio::main]
369	async fn main() {
370	    console_subscriber::init(); // Replaces the default tracing subscriber
371	    // ... rest of your application
372	}
373	```
374	
375	Then in another terminal:
376	
377	```bash
378	$ RUSTFLAGS="--cfg tokio_unstable" cargo run   # Required compile-time flag
379	$ tokio-console                                # Connects to 127.0.0.1:6669
380	```
381	
382	#### tracing + #[instrument]: Structured Logging for Async
383	
384	The [`tracing`](https://docs.rs/tracing) crate understands `Future` lifetimes. Spans stay open across `.await` points, giving you a logical call stack even when the OS thread has moved on:
385	
386	```rust
387	use tracing::{info, instrument};
388	
389	#[instrument(skip(db_pool), fields(user_id = %user_id))]
390	async fn handle_request(user_id: u64, db_pool: &Pool) -> Result<Response> {
391	    info!("looking up user");
392	    let user = db_pool.get_user(user_id).await?;  // span stays open across .await
393	    info!(email = %user.email, "found user");
394	    let orders = fetch_orders(user_id).await?;     // still the same span
395	    Ok(build_response(user, orders))
396	}
397	```
398	
399	Output (with `tracing_subscriber::fmt::json()`):
400	
401	```json
402	{"timestamp":"...","level":"INFO","span":{"name":"handle_request","user_id":"42"},"message":"looking up user"}
403	{"timestamp":"...","level":"INFO","span":{"name":"handle_request","user_id":"42"},"fields":{"email":"a@b.com"},"message":"found user"}
404	```
405	
406	#### Debugging Checklist
407	
408	| Symptom | Likely Cause | Tool |
409	|---------|-------------|------|
410	| Task hangs forever | Missing `.await` or deadlocked `Mutex` | `tokio-console` task view |
411	| Low throughput | Blocking call on async thread | `tokio-console` poll-time histogram |
412	| `Future is not Send` | Non-Send type held across `.await` | Compiler error + `#[instrument]` to locate |
413	| Mysterious cancellation | Parent `select!` dropped a branch | `tracing` span lifecycle events |
414	
415	> **Tip**: Enable `RUSTFLAGS="--cfg tokio_unstable"` to get task-level metrics
416	> in tokio-console. This is a compile-time flag, not a runtime one.
417	
418	### Testing Async Code
419	
420	Async code introduces unique testing challenges — you need a runtime, time control, and strategies for testing concurrent behavior.
421	
422	**Basic async tests** with `#[tokio::test]`:
423	
424	```rust
425	// Cargo.toml
426	// [dev-dependencies]
427	// tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full", "test-util"] }
428	
429	#[tokio::test]
430	async fn test_basic_async() {
431	    let result = fetch_data().await;
432	    assert_eq!(result, "expected");
433	}
434	
435	// Single-threaded test (useful for !Send types):
436	#[tokio::test(flavor = "current_thread")]
437	async fn test_single_threaded() {
438	    let rc = std::rc::Rc::new(42);
439	    let val = async { *rc }.await;
440	    assert_eq!(val, 42);
441	}
442	
443	// Multi-threaded with explicit worker count:
444	#[tokio::test(flavor = "multi_thread", worker_threads = 2)]
445	async fn test_concurrent_behavior() {
446	    // Tests race conditions with real concurrency
447	    let counter = std::sync::Arc::new(std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32::new(0));
448	    let c1 = counter.clone();
449	    let c2 = counter.clone();
450	    let (a, b) = tokio::join!(
451	        tokio::spawn(async move { c1.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::SeqCst) }),
452	        tokio::spawn(async move { c2.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::SeqCst) }),
453	    );
454	    a.unwrap();
455	    b.unwrap();
456	    assert_eq!(counter.load(std::sync::atomic::Ordering::SeqCst), 2);
457	}
458	```
459	
460	**Time manipulation** — test timeouts without actually waiting:
461	
462	```rust
463	use tokio::time::{self, Duration, Instant};
464	
465	#[tokio::test]
466	async fn test_timeout_behavior() {
467	    // Pause time — sleep() advances instantly, no real wall-clock delay
468	    time::pause();
469	
470	    let start = Instant::now();
471	    time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3600)).await; // "waits" 1 hour — takes 0ms
472	    assert!(start.elapsed() >= Duration::from_secs(3600));
473	    // Test ran in milliseconds, not an hour!
474	}
475	
476	#[tokio::test]
477	async fn test_retry_timing() {
478	    time::pause();
479	
480	    // Test that our retry logic waits the expected durations
481	    let start = Instant::now();
482	    let result = retry_with_backoff(|| async {
483	        Err::<(), _>("simulated failure")
484	    }, 3, Duration::from_secs(1))
485	    .await;
486	
487	    assert!(result.is_err());
488	    // 1s + 2s + 4s = 7s of backoff (exponential)
489	    assert!(start.elapsed() >= Duration::from_secs(7));
490	}
491	
492	#[tokio::test]
493	async fn test_deadline_exceeded() {
494	    time::pause();
495	
496	    let result = tokio::time::timeout(
497	        Duration::from_secs(5),
498	        async {
499	            // Simulate slow operation
500	            time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(10)).await;
501	            "done"
502	        }
503	    ).await;
504	
505	    assert!(result.is_err()); // Timed out
506	}
507	```
508	
509	**Mocking async dependencies** — use trait objects or generics:
510	
511	```rust
512	// Define a trait for the dependency:
513	trait Storage {
514	    async fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String>;
515	    async fn set(&self, key: &str, value: String);
516	}
517	
518	// Production implementation:
519	struct RedisStorage { /* ... */ }
520	impl Storage for RedisStorage {
521	    async fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String> {
522	        // Real Redis call
523	        todo!()
524	    }
525	    async fn set(&self, key: &str, value: String) {
526	        todo!()
527	    }
528	}
529	
530	// Test mock:
531	struct MockStorage {
532	    data: std::sync::Mutex<std::collections::HashMap<String, String>>,
533	}
534	
535	impl MockStorage {
536	    fn new() -> Self {
537	        MockStorage { data: std::sync::Mutex::new(std::collections::HashMap::new()) }
538	    }
539	}
540	
541	impl Storage for MockStorage {
542	    async fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String> {
543	        self.data.lock().unwrap().get(key).cloned()
544	    }
545	    async fn set(&self, key: &str, value: String) {
546	        self.data.lock().unwrap().insert(key.to_string(), value);
547	    }
548	}
549	
550	// Tested function is generic over Storage:
551	async fn cache_lookup<S: Storage>(store: &S, key: &str) -> String {
552	    match store.get(key).await {
553	        Some(val) => val,
554	        None => {
555	            let val = "computed".to_string();
556	            store.set(key, val.clone()).await;
557	            val
558	        }
559	    }
560	}
561	
562	#[tokio::test]
563	async fn test_cache_miss_then_hit() {
564	    let mock = MockStorage::new();
565	
566	    // First call: miss → computes and stores
567	    let val = cache_lookup(&mock, "key1").await;
568	    assert_eq!(val, "computed");
569	
570	    // Second call: hit → returns stored value
571	    let val = cache_lookup(&mock, "key1").await;
572	    assert_eq!(val, "computed");
573	    assert!(mock.data.lock().unwrap().contains_key("key1"));
574	}
575	```
576	
577	**Testing channels and task communication**:
578	
579	```rust
580	#[tokio::test]
581	async fn test_producer_consumer() {
582	    let (tx, mut rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(10);
583	
584	    tokio::spawn(async move {
585	        for i in 0..5 {
586	            tx.send(i).await.unwrap();
587	        }
588	        // tx dropped here — channel closes
589	    });
590	
591	    let mut received = Vec::new();
592	    while let Some(val) = rx.recv().await {
593	        received.push(val);
594	    }
595	
596	    assert_eq!(received, vec![0, 1, 2, 3, 4]);
597	}
598	```
599	
600	| Test Pattern | When to Use | Key Tool |
601	|-------------|-------------|----------|
602	| `#[tokio::test]` | All async tests | `tokio = { features = ["macros", "rt"] }` |
603	| `time::pause()` | Testing timeouts, retries, periodic tasks | `tokio::time::pause()` |
604	| Trait mocking | Testing business logic without I/O | Generic `<S: Storage>` |
605	| `current_thread` flavor | Testing `!Send` types or deterministic scheduling | `#[tokio::test(flavor = "current_thread")]` |
606	| `multi_thread` flavor | Testing race conditions | `#[tokio::test(flavor = "multi_thread")]` |
607	
608	> **Key Takeaways — Common Pitfalls**
609	> - Never block the executor — use `spawn_blocking` for CPU/sync work
610	> - Never hold a `MutexGuard` across `.await` — scope locks tightly or use `tokio::sync::Mutex`
611	> - Cancellation drops the future instantly — use "cancel-safe" patterns for partial operations
612	> - Use `tokio-console` and `#[tracing::instrument]` for debugging async code
613	> - Test async code with `#[tokio::test]` and `time::pause()` for deterministic timing
614	
615	> **See also:** [Ch 8 — Tokio Deep Dive](ch08-tokio-deep-dive.md) for sync primitives, [Ch 13 — Production Patterns](ch13-production-patterns.md) for graceful shutdown and structured concurrency
616	
617	***
